The '''Koenigsegg Regera''' is a limited production, [[plug-in hybrid]] grand touring [[sports car]] manufactured by Swedish automotive manufacturer [[Koenigsegg]]. It was unveiled at the March 2015 [[Geneva Motor Show#2015|Geneva Motor Show]]. The name {{lang|sv|Regera}} is a Swedish verb, meaning "to reign" or "to rule". Koenigsegg produced 85 Regeras,<ref>{{Cite web |title=How Many Koenigseggs Are There? |url=https://www.eggregistry.com/blog/how-many-koenigseggs-are-there |access-date=6 August 2023 |website=Egg Registry |language=en-CA}}</ref> most of which were sold upon unveiling.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.topgear.com/car-news/supercars/the-koenigsegg-regera-hypercar-has-sold-out-80-units |title=The Koenigsegg Regera Has Sold Out |first=Vijay |last=Pattni |work=Top Gear |date=13 June 2017 |access-date=16 March 2018 |archive-date=16 March 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180316152123/https://www.topgear.com/car-news/supercars/the-koenigsegg-regera-hypercar-has-sold-out-80-units |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="CarAndDriver-2015-03-06">{{Cite news |title=2016 Koenigsegg Regera: 1500 Horsepower, Single-Speed Gearbox – Official Photos and Info |url=http://www.caranddriver.com/news/2016-koenigsegg-regera-photos-and-info-news |date=6 March 2015 |access-date=23 April 2015 |archive-date=24 November 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181124072124/https://www.caranddriver.com/news/2016-koenigsegg-regera-photos-and-info-news |url-status=live }}</ref>

The Regera was developed and designed to be a more practical, luxurious, [[Grand tourer|grand touring]] alternative to the rest of Koenigsegg's lightweight sports car lineup: initially the [[Koenigsegg Agera|Agera]] and later the [[Koenigsegg Jesko|Jesko]]. Consequently it is focused on the smooth and instant delivery of power provided by its overhauled powertrain, rather than on-track performance. 

The introduction of the Regera alongside the [[Koenigsegg Agera#Koenigsegg Agera RS (2015–2018)|Agera RS]] in 2015 resulted in Koenigsegg for the first time simultaneously having two models in production.<ref name="Koenigsegg-website">{{Cite web |title = Regera – Koenigsegg |url = http://koenigsegg.com/regera/ |access-date = 23 April 2015 |archive-date = 20 October 2016 |archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20161020074346/http://koenigsegg.com/regera/ |url-status = dead }}</ref> This role was passed from the Agera to the Jesko in 2019, which briefly shared the production line with the Regera when Jesko production began in late 2021.
{{Listen|type=sound|filename=Regera Goodwood 2018.ogg|title=Koenigsegg Regera accelerating}}

== Conception ==
Koenigsegg founder [[Christian von Koenigsegg]] purchased a [[Tesla Model S|Tesla Model S P85+]] in 2013 and was impressed by the car's ability to instantly deliver power without the need for a downshift or to wait out [[Turbocharger#Turbocharger lag|turbo lag]].<ref name=MrJWWvid>{{cite AV media |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N9ekHJ1PrR4 |title=NEW Koenigsegg Regera FIRST DRIVE In The £1.5M Hypercar! |via=YouTube |date=19 July 2018 |access-date=8 August 2018 |archive-date=19 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190519140733/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N9ekHJ1PrR4 |url-status=live }}</ref> He was especially impressed with the direct and intuitive experience provided by an [[electric car]] like the Tesla, remarking that the instantaneous response of the motors was something even a [[Formula One car]] could not attain with a traditional [[internal combustion engine]].<ref name=CvKAMA>{{cite press release|url=https://www.koenigsegg.com/ama-with-cvk-part-4/|title=AMA WITH CVK – PART 4 – REGERA AND THE FUTURE|publisher=Koenigsegg Automotive|date=7 September 2015|access-date=8 August 2018|archive-date=15 September 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150915042340/https://www.koenigsegg.com/ama-with-cvk-part-4/|url-status=dead}}</ref> Von Koenigsegg sought to combine this desirable aspect of electric powertrains with the traditional Koenigsegg experience of a lightweight, powerful sports car.

Realizing some form of compromise was necessary, von Koenigsegg decided to look to a hybrid solution. By removing the traditional 7-speed [[dual-clutch transmission]] and switching to a single-gear ''direct-drive'' system, Koenigsegg mitigated the added weight of the three electric motors and a 4.5&nbsp;kW·h battery pack installed in the Regera as compared to the previous Agera models.<ref name=CvKAMA />

=== Development ===
[[File:Nvdia Holodeck Regera.png|thumb|The Regera in NVIDIA Holodeck]]
The Regera was designed with a combination of the [[CATIA]] software suite and [[Autodesk Inventor]], with [[AutoCAD|AutoCAD Electrical]] being primarily used to redesign the vehicle's electrical systems from past Koenigsegg models.<ref name="autodesk">{{Cite web|url=https://damassets.autodesk.net/content/dam/autodesk/www/campaigns/mfg-webinar/autodesk-customer_brochure-a4_en_2015.pdf|title=Perfecting the Megacar: Electric transmission – Koenigsegg's new agenda|website=Autodesk|access-date=30 April 2018|archive-date=1 May 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180501005150/https://damassets.autodesk.net/content/dam/autodesk/www/campaigns/mfg-webinar/autodesk-customer_brochure-a4_en_2015.pdf|url-status=live}}</ref> According to Koenigsegg Technical Director Jon Gunner, the Regera makes use of several [[Node (circuits)|power nodes]] to connect wiring throughout the car compared to the single power node of previous models, which saves weight.<ref name="autodesk" />

Koenigsegg uses [[3D printing]] technologies to manufacture many parts of the Regera. This includes static components like details in the interior of the car under the [[upholstery]] and the large electrical exhaust port at the rear of the car, as well as dynamic components like the [[variable-geometry turbocharger]] in the engine.<ref name="autodesk" /><ref>{{Cite news|url=https://3dprintingindustry.com/news/3d-printing-automotive-industry-2-82838/|title=Top 10 3D printed automotive industry innovations available right now|last=Hall|first=Nick|date=20 June 2016|access-date=30 April 2018|language=en-US|archive-date=1 May 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180501092820/https://3dprintingindustry.com/news/3d-printing-automotive-industry-2-82838/|url-status=live}}</ref> This manufacturing technique allowed Koenigsegg to better tune airflow through the turbochargers and achieve a more complex inner housing shape which was impossible with conventional methods.

The Regera was used by [[Nvidia|NVIDIA]] to introduce Project Holodeck, a [[virtual reality]] environment capable of importing and manipulating high-detail 3D models. This technology was used by Koenigsegg engineers to collaborate on design ideas in real-time and could allow customers to experience their custom made vehicle in VR before it is purchased or built.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/2017/05/10/holodeck/|title=NVIDIA Reveals Holodeck, Its Groundbreaking Project for Photorealistic, Collaborative VR|last=Weinstein|first=David|date=10 May 2017|work=The Official NVIDIA Blog|access-date=30 April 2018|publisher=Nvidia|language=en-US|archive-date=1 May 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180501005006/https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/2017/05/10/holodeck/|url-status=live}}</ref>
{{clear}}

==Specifications==
The Regera is a two-door [[targa top]] with a detachable roof that can be stowed in the [[Trunk (car)|boot]].<ref name=tgreview>{{cite web|url=https://www.topgear.com/car-news/big-reads/world-exclusive-drive-koenigsegg-regera|title=We drive Sweden's utterly bonkers 1479bhp, 1465lb ft, 250mph hybrid hypercar|first=Ollie|last=Marriage|work=Top Gear |date=17 August 2016|access-date=16 March 2018|archive-date=17 March 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180317102130/https://www.topgear.com/car-news/big-reads/world-exclusive-drive-koenigsegg-regera|url-status=live}}</ref> It is the first hybrid car to be produced by Koenigsegg, as well as the first vehicle to use their ''Direct-Drive'' System for power delivery. As the company's '[[Grand tourer|grand touring]]' oriented offer in their two-car lineup, the Regera focuses more on interior luxury and daily usability instead of hardcore track performance like the [[Koenigsegg Jesko|Jesko]].<ref>{{Cite AV media |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DBNt28idyqk&t=7s |title=Koenigsegg Regera Under the Skin – /INSIDE KOENIGSEGG |publisher=The Drive |via=YouTube |date=12 October 2016 |access-date=14 March 2018 |archive-date=18 April 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230418113758/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DBNt28idyqk&t=7s |url-status=live }}</ref>

===Powertrain===
[[File:Koenigsegg Regera powertrain.png|thumb|Diagram of the Regera's powertrain]]
[[File:Regera power diagram.png|thumb|Power diagram of the Regera's ICE and hybrid system]]

The Regera has a reported total power output of {{convert|1822|PS|kW hp PS|abbr=on|0|order=out}} through a hybrid powertrain. As in general, the Regera's internal combustion engine (ICE) generates its greatest power only at high rpm; however, due to the fixed gear, this corresponds to very high speeds. Power at low speeds is filled in by the electric motors, giving a maximum combined mechanical power output of {{convert|1,500|hp|kW hp PS|abbr=on|0|order=out}} and {{convert|1475|lbft|abbr=on|order=flip|0}} of torque.<ref name="Koenigsegg-website" /><ref>{{Cite web|title = First Look At Koenigsegg Regera: 1,500 HP From Direct Drive System| date=3 March 2015 |url = http://www.motorauthority.com/news/1097066_first-look-at-koenigsegg-regera-1500-hp-from-direct-drive-system|access-date = 23 April 2015|archive-date = 11 March 2015|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20150311182749/http://www.motorauthority.com/news/1097066_first-look-at-koenigsegg-regera-1500-hp-from-direct-drive-system|url-status = live}}</ref><ref name="Jalopnik-2015-04-23">{{Cite web|title = How The 1,500 HP Koenigsegg Regera Hits 248 MPH Without A Gearbox|url = http://jalopnik.com/how-the-1-500-hp-koenigsegg-regera-hits-248-mph-without-1689181377|access-date = 23 April 2015|first = Travis|last = Okulski|date = 3 March 2015|archive-date = 11 March 2015|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20150311225255/http://jalopnik.com/how-the-1-500-hp-koenigsegg-regera-hits-248-mph-without-1689181377|url-status = live}}</ref>

==== Internal combustion engine ====
[[File:Koenigsegg Regera, GIMS 2019, Le Grand-Saconnex (GIMS1053).jpg|thumb|Regera engine and exhaust system on display at [[Geneva International Motor Show]]]]

The Internal Combustion Engine (ICE) is a mid-rear mounted, in-house developed, [[twin-turbocharged]] [[V8 engine]] with a 5.0-litre displacement. It has four valves per cylinder, each with a bore and stroke of {{convert|92|x|95.25|mm|2|abbr=in}} and a [[compression ratio]] of 9.3:1. The engine utilizes [[dry sump]] lubrication and [[Overhead camshaft#Dual overhead camshaft|dual overhead camshafts]], and has a cylinder bank angle of 90º.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.the-lowdown.com/koenigsegg-regera-feature/?v=7516fd43adaa|title=A Closer Look at the Koenigsegg Regera|publisher=The-Lowdown|date=31 July 2015|access-date=16 March 2018|archive-date=2 May 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230502224233/https://www.the-lowdown.com/koenigsegg-regera-feature/?v=322b26af01d5|url-status=live}}</ref> It is rated at {{convert|820|kW|hp PS|abbr=on|0}} at 7,800&nbsp;rpm and {{convert|1280|Nm|lbft|abbr=on|0}} of torque at 4,100&nbsp;rpm on 95 octane RON gasoline. A [[Flexible-fuel vehicle|flex fuel]] sensor is installed in the fuel tank to detect [[E85|E85 biofuel]], which increases the power output to {{convert|1500|hp|disp=flip|sigfig=3|abbr=on}}.<ref>{{cite web |title=Regera Technical Specification |url=http://koenigseggci-prod.northeurope.azurecontainer.io/technical-specifications-regera |website=Koenigsegg.com |access-date=18 April 2023 |ref=tech-specs |archive-date=18 April 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230418002937/http://koenigseggci-prod.northeurope.azurecontainer.io/technical-specifications-regera |url-status=live }}</ref> The ICE is rev-limited at 8,250&nbsp;rpm.<ref name="motorauthority">{{Cite web|title = Christian von Koenigsegg brings the Regera to Jay Leno's Garage|url = https://www.motorauthority.com/news/1118701_christian-von-koenigsegg-brings-the-regera-to-jay-lenos-garage|website = MotorAuthority|date = 11 September 2018|access-date = 13 September 2018|first = Viknesh|last = Vijayenthiran|archive-date = 13 September 2018|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20180913112502/https://www.motorauthority.com/news/1118701_christian-von-koenigsegg-brings-the-regera-to-jay-lenos-garage|url-status = live}}</ref>

==== Electric motors ====
The ICE works in conjunction with three [[YASA Limited|YASA]] electric motors with a total capacity of {{convert|520|kW|PS hp|0|abbr=on}} and {{convert|900|Nm|lbft|abbr=on|0}} of torque. One {{convert|215|hp|kW hp PS|abbr=on|0|order=out}} YASA 400 Motor Generator Unit mounted on the crankshaft acts as starter and generator and supplies torque fill; and two {{convert|241|hp|kW hp PS|abbr=on|0|order=out}} YASA 750 wheel-shaft mounted electric motors drive each rear wheel and provide [[torque vectoring]] and manages traction.<ref>{{Cite news|url=http://www.yasa.com/news/yasa-powers-record-breaking-hypercar/|title=YASA and the 1500 bhp Koenigsegg Regera – YASA Limited|date=10 March 2015|work=YASA Limited|access-date=14 March 2018|language=en-US|archive-date=15 March 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180315003739/http://www.yasa.com/news/yasa-powers-record-breaking-hypercar/|url-status=live}}</ref> The electric motors are powered by a 4.5{{Nbsp}}kWh, 800 volt, {{Convert|75|kg|lb|abbr=unit}} liquid cooled battery pack developed by [[Rimac Automobili]], making it the first 800-volt production car.<ref>{{cite press release|url=http://www.rimac-automobili.com/en/press/releases/rimac-helps-bring-world-s-most-powerful-production-car-to-reality/|title=Rimac Helps Bring World's Most Powerful Production Car To Reality|publisher=Rimac Automobili|date=3 March 2015|access-date=16 March 2018|archive-date=22 June 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180622140226/http://www.rimac-automobili.com/en/press/releases/rimac-helps-bring-world-s-most-powerful-production-car-to-reality/|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.thedrive.com/new-cars/2404/koenigseggs-regera-is-the-worlds-most-endearing-instrument-of-chaos|title=Koenigsegg's Regera Is the World's Most Endearing Instrument of Chaos|first=Will|last=Courtney|date=2 March 2016|access-date=16 March 2018|archive-date=16 March 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180316152308/http://www.thedrive.com/new-cars/2404/koenigseggs-regera-is-the-worlds-most-endearing-instrument-of-chaos|url-status=live}}</ref> Koenigsegg claims that the battery pack is the most power-dense battery pack ever created for a production car.<ref name="CarAndDriver-2015-03-06"/><ref name="Koenigsegg-website" /><ref name="Jalopnik-2015-04-23"/><ref>{{Cite web|title = Koenigsegg unveils 1100kW Regera|url = http://www.drive.com.au/motor-news/koenigsegg-unveils-1100kw-regera-20150304-13ujz8.html|access-date = 23 April 2015|archive-date = 5 March 2015|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20150305115424/http://www.drive.com.au/motor-news/koenigsegg-unveils-1100kw-regera-20150304-13ujz8.html|url-status = live}}</ref>

Under braking, the [[kinetic energy recovery system]] in the Regera can regenerate up to 150{{Nbsp}}kW of power to recharge the battery during operation.<ref>{{Cite press release |title=Rimac Helps Bring World's Most Powerful Production Car to Reality |url=http://www.rimac-automobili.com/en/press/releases/rimac-helps-bring-world-s-most-powerful-production-car-to-reality/ |publisher=Rimac |date=3 March 2015 |access-date=13 April 2018 |archive-date=22 June 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180622140226/http://www.rimac-automobili.com/en/press/releases/rimac-helps-bring-world-s-most-powerful-production-car-to-reality/ |url-status=dead }}</ref>

===Transmission===
Christian von Koenigsegg invented the ''Koenigsegg Direct-Drive'' System (KDD), and it was developed for the Regera by the Koenigsegg Advanced Engineering Team.<ref name=newatlas1>{{cite web|url=https://newatlas.com/koenigsegg-regera-geneva/36376/|title=Koenigsegg creates a new breed of hyper-hybrid with 1,500-hp, transmission-less Regera|publisher=New Atlas|first=Chris|last=Weiss|date=17 March 2015|access-date=10 September 2018|archive-date=12 August 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160812181132/http://newatlas.com/koenigsegg-regera-geneva/36376/|url-status=live}}</ref> The KDD system effectively eliminates the need for a transmission and allows for pure EV (electric vehicle) mode. The Regera does not have a traditional multi-gear transmission but instead features a single-speed fixed-gear transmission, often called a ''[[direct drive mechanism|direct-drive]]'', with a 2.73:1 reduction ratio, meaning the crankshaft mounted on the ICE will rotate 2.73 times for every 1 time that the output shaft of the ''direct-drive'' mechanism will rotate.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.roadandtrack.com/new-cars/future-cars/videos/a26013/christian-von-koenigsegg-drives-new-regera-supercar/|title=Watch the New Koenigsegg Regera Supercar In Motion|last=Kierstein|first=Alex|date=7 July 2015|website=Road & Track|access-date=2 August 2019|archive-date=15 May 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190515080258/https://www.roadandtrack.com/new-cars/future-cars/videos/a26013/christian-von-koenigsegg-drives-new-regera-supercar/|url-status=live}}</ref>

At speeds below {{Convert|30.|mph|km/h|abbr=unit}}, the wheel shaft electric motors mostly propel the car through the use of a [[Fluid coupling|hydraulic coupling]] that lets the ICE and the crankshaft mounted electric motor slip. In reverse, only the wheel shaft electric motors propel the car.<ref name="CarAndDriver-2015-03-06" /><ref>{{cite AV media |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AMmdl77VOxg |title=First Look: Koenigsegg Regera with Christian von Koenigsegg |via=YouTube |date=17 January 2016 |access-date=23 February 2018 |archive-date=5 April 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180405211328/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AMmdl77VOxg |url-status=live }}</ref> Above 30{{Nbsp}}mph, the RPM of the crankshaft mounted electric motor and internal combustion engine are proportional to wheel speed through locking of the hydraulic coupling.<ref name="CarAndDriver-2015-03-06" /> However, Christian von Koenigsegg described and demonstrated how the Regera features a steering-wheel-mounted paddle that simulates a traditional downshift by initiating slip of the hydraulic coupling connecting the crankshaft to the output shaft. This results in higher RPM of the ICE and the crankshaft mounted electric motor and therefore higher power at lower speeds than what would have been possible if the coupling would have been locked at all times.<ref>{{Cite AV media |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lIEd20OZ0RA |title=First Driving Footage – Koenigsegg Regera |publisher=Koenigsegg |via=YouTube |date=12 January 2015 |access-date=6 March 2018 |archive-date=15 August 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170815222120/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lIEd20OZ0RA |url-status=live }}</ref> Koenigsegg reported that the omission of a gearbox and addition of electric motors and battery only added {{Convert|88|kg|lb|abbr=unit}} compared to what the Regera would have weighed with the same combustion engine but a 7-speed dual-clutch transmission (DCT) and no electric motors or batteries.<ref name="Koenigsegg-website" /><ref>{{Cite web |title=Koenigsegg Will Sell You A Car Without A Transmission For $1.9 Million |url=http://jalopnik.com/koenigsegg-will-sell-you-a-car-without-a-transmission-f-1689101650 |access-date=23 April 2015 |first=Máté |last=Petrány |date=3 March 2015 |archive-date=18 April 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150418215221/http://jalopnik.com/koenigsegg-will-sell-you-a-car-without-a-transmission-f-1689101650 |url-status=live }}</ref>

=== Suspension ===
[[File:Regera ICE brightened cropped.jpg|thumb|'Triplex' damper system|left]]

The Regera features a similar suspension layout to the preceding Agera models, with [[Double wishbone suspension|double wishbone]] suspension at the front and rear axles.<ref name=supercarblog>{{Cite web|url=https://www.thesupercarblog.com/jay-leno-drives-the-koenigsegg-regera-comes-out-mighty-impressed/|title=Jay Leno Drives the Koenigsegg Regera, Comes Out Mighty Impressed|last=Siddiqui|first=Zaid|date=14 September 2018|website=TheSupercarBlog.com|access-date=19 September 2018|archive-date=18 September 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180918123622/https://www.thesupercarblog.com/jay-leno-drives-the-koenigsegg-regera-comes-out-mighty-impressed/|url-status=live}}</ref> The wishbones are manufactured from chrome-molybdenum steel tubes, which was chosen over carbon-fibre due to the significant cost and complexity of the latter option.<ref name=regerablog2>{{cite press release|url=https://www.koenigsegg.com/build-7128-subframe-and-suspension/|title=BUILD 7128 – SUBFRAME AND SUSPENSION|publisher=Koenigsegg Automotive|date=13 December 2016|access-date=19 September 2018|archive-date=15 December 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161215152841/https://www.koenigsegg.com/build-7128-subframe-and-suspension/|url-status=dead}}</ref> The suspension incorporates special Z-shaped [[anti-roll bar]]s that connect to the body at only one point (as opposed to two for traditional U-shaped bars). Also returning from the Agera models is Koenigsegg's 'Triplex' damper system, which consists of three custom [[Öhlins]] [[shock absorber]]s on the rear suspension subsystem.<ref name=regerablog2/> While [[Suspension (vehicle)#Anti-dive and anti-squat|anti-dive]] forces under heavy braking are achieved by obliquely mounting the top wishbones in the front of the car, this mounting position could not be achieved in the rear. Therefore, the purpose of the third 'Triplex' rear damper is to provide anti-squat forces under hard acceleration.
{{clear}}

===Wheels===
The Regera has aluminium wheels or carbon fibre wheels (optional) with diameters of {{convert|19|in|disp=flip}} at the front and {{convert|20|in|disp=flip}} at the rear with [[Centerlock wheel|center-lock wheel nuts]]. The tyres are [[Michelin]] Pilot Sport 4S' with codes of 275/35 ZR 19 (100Y) for the front and 345/30 ZR 20 (106Y) for the rear.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.automobile-catalog.com/tire/2017/2534780/koenigsegg_regera.html|title=2017 Koenigsegg Regera tire and wheel sizes|publisher=Automobile-Catalog|access-date=16 March 2018}}</ref> The car comes with track focused Michelin Pilot Sport Cup 2 tyres when equipped with the Ghost package. The brakes are ventilated [[Ceramic matrix composite|carbon-ceramic]] discs, with a diameter of {{convert|397|mm|in|1|abbr=on}} and six-piston calipers at the front along with a diameter of {{convert|380|mm|in|1|abbr=on}} and four-piston calipers at the rear.<ref name="CarAndDriver-2015-03-06"/>

=== Aerodynamics ===
[[File:Regera aero highlights.png|thumb|A Regera equipped with the optional 'Ghost' aerodynamic package (contents of the package highlighted for illustrative purposes)]]

Like its other production counterparts, the Regera is equipped with active aerodynamics for optimized downforce. At the rear is a foldable hydraulic wing, and at the front are active flaps that can modify the flow of air under the car. The manufacturer claims that the stock aerodynamic system is capable of generating {{convert|450|kg|lb|abbr=on}} of downforce at {{convert|250|km/h|mph|0|abbr=on}}.<ref name="Koenigsegg-website" />

Before the [[Geneva Motor Show#2018|2018 Geneva Motor Show]], Koenigsegg revealed the optional 'Ghost' package for the Regera. This package extends the front splitter and the side sills, adds canards to the front fenders, and adds fixed winglets to the rear quarter panels.<ref name="MotorAuthority-2018-03-05">{{Cite web|title = Koenigsegg Regera gets Ghost high-downforce package|url = https://www.motorauthority.com/news/1115587_koenigsegg-regera-gets-ghost-high-downforce-package|access-date = 16 March 2018|date = 5 March 2018|publisher = MotorAuthority|first = Viknesh|last = Vijayenthiran|archive-date = 16 March 2018|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20180316151943/https://www.motorauthority.com/news/1115587_koenigsegg-regera-gets-ghost-high-downforce-package|url-status = live}}</ref> Koenigsegg claims that, with the Ghost package installed, the downforce of the Regera is "increased by more than 20 percent".<ref name="MotorAuthority-2018-03-05" />

===Interior features===
[[File:2017-03-07 Geneva Motor Show 1270.JPG|thumb|Interior with roboticized [[List of cars with non-standard door designs#Dihedral doors|dihedral doors]] in open position|left]]

The Regera includes added insulation to help add to the luxury by reducing cabin noise caused by the wind and powertrain along with 8-way electrically adjustable memory foam seats included as an added luxury item over previous models. The Regera also includes many new technical features such as a [[Neonode]] 9-inch infrared infotainment system, a complete camera system with recording capabilities (front, inner, and rear), [[CarPlay|Apple CarPlay]], a new sound system, ambient lighting, and 4G and Wi-Fi connectivity.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.neonode.com/neonode-zforce-touchscreen-in-koenigsegg-regera-at-geneva-motor/|title=Neonode zForce® Touchscreen in Koenigsegg Regera at Geneva|date=27 February 2015|website=Neonode|access-date=25 March 2018|archive-date=1 April 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190401015123/https://www.neonode.com/neonode-zforce-touchscreen-in-koenigsegg-regera-at-geneva-motor/|url-status=dead}}</ref> It also features front and rear [[parking sensor]]s.<ref name="Koenigsegg-website"/><ref>{{Cite web|url = http://www.digitaltrends.com/cars/koenigsegg-regera-official-pictures-specs-and-performance/|title = Koenigsegg's Regera goes green with a 1,500-HP plug-in hybrid powertrain|website = Digital Trends|last = Edelstein|first = Stephen|date = 3 March 2015|access-date = 23 April 2015|archive-date = 23 April 2015|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20150423141427/http://www.digitaltrends.com/cars/koenigsegg-regera-official-pictures-specs-and-performance/|url-status = live}}</ref>

The wireless connectivity capabilities of the Regera allow for Koenigsegg to send over-the-air firmware and software updates to each car as necessary.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://carbuzz.com/news/koenigsegg-regera-over-the-air-updates-starting-this-summer|title=Koenigsegg Regera Over-The-Air Updates Starting This Summer|work=CarBuzz|access-date=20 March 2018|language=en-us|archive-date=2 May 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230502224225/https://carbuzz.com/news/koenigsegg-regera-over-the-air-updates-starting-this-summer|url-status=live}}</ref> This allows for the remote modification of parameters such as the [[power band]] of the electric motors or throttle response without the need for physical work on the car.<ref name="tgreview" />

The Regera has active engine mounts for the ICE and transmission to rest on. The engine mounts are designed to stay soft at lower speeds, significantly reducing vibrations and engine noise, adding to the luxury effect of the vehicle. While driving more aggressively or at higher speeds, the engine mounts will firm up, giving greater lateral response to the driver.<ref name="Koenigsegg-website"/>

=== Exterior features ===
The [[Daytime running lamp|day-time running lamps]] on the Regera were designed to resemble a constellation, with the carbon-fibre background resembling the night sky. In addition, the headlamps can actively vent out condensation through two small fans installed in each lamp.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://jalopnik.com/the-amazing-tech-that-makes-the-koenigsegg-regera-a-hyp-1762221011|title=The Amazing Tech That Makes The Koenigsegg Regera A Hyperfast Hybrid Spaceship On Wheels|publisher=Jalopnik|first=David|last=Tracy|date=1 March 2016|access-date=16 March 2018|archive-date=17 March 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180317035741/https://jalopnik.com/the-amazing-tech-that-makes-the-koenigsegg-regera-a-hyp-1762221011|url-status=live}}</ref> Koenigsegg claims that the Regera is the first "fully-robotized" car.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.roadandtrack.com/new-cars/videos/a27329/koenigsegg-regera-autoskin-robot-body-panels/|title=Check Out the Koenigsegg Regera's Transformers-Style Robotic Body Panels|last=Woodard|first=Collin|date=12 November 2015|work=Road & Track|access-date=25 April 2018|language=en-US|archive-date=26 April 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180426080121/https://www.roadandtrack.com/new-cars/videos/a27329/koenigsegg-regera-autoskin-robot-body-panels/|url-status=live}}</ref> All openings and wing mirrors are fitted with proximity sensors to safeguard against the doors striking nearby objects (curb, low roof, etc.) while opening or closing.<ref>{{cite web |title=The first fully robotized car |url=https://www.koenigsegg.com/car/regera/ |website=koenigsegg.com |access-date=21 May 2020 |archive-date=12 June 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180612163224/https://www.koenigsegg.com/regera/ |url-status=dead }}</ref> The vehicle features hydraulic pumps and accumulators to control the active front and rear wings, as well as active chassis control and lifting. Hydraulic lifters were added to the already existing pumps and accumulators to allow for all body panels on the vehicle to be remotely operated.<ref name="Koenigsegg-website"/>

The Regera uses a fishtail-style dual exhaust system designed by [[Akrapovič]] constructed of titanium, [[Inconel]], and stainless steel.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.akrapovic.com/#!/news/27550/akrapovic-and-koenigsegg-unite-for-a-world-first|title=Akrapovič and Koenigsegg Unite for a World First|date=2 March 2015|website=Akrapovič|access-date=23 March 2018|archive-date=24 March 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180324041741/https://www.akrapovic.com/#!/news/27550/akrapovic-and-koenigsegg-unite-for-a-world-first|url-status=live}}</ref> The large central exhaust pipe under the charging port is not used to vent exhaust from the ICE, rather, it expels hot air from the cooling system for the batteries and electronics in the car.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://money.cnn.com/2015/03/04/autos/koenigsegg-regera-super-hybrid/index.html|title=Koenigsegg's super hybrid: Zero to 248 in less than 20 seconds|last=Valdes-Dapena|first=Chris Isidore and Peter|work=CNNMoney|access-date=23 March 2018|archive-date=27 April 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190427134405/https://money.cnn.com/2015/03/04/autos/koenigsegg-regera-super-hybrid/index.html|url-status=dead}}</ref>

===Performance===
Koenigsegg states it is capable of accelerating to {{convert|100|km/h|mph|0|abbr=on}} from a stand still in 2.8 seconds, {{convert|200|km/h|mph|0|abbr=on}} in 6.6 seconds, {{convert|300|km/h|mph|0|abbr=on}} in 10.9 seconds and attaining a speed of {{convert|400|km/h|mph|0|abbr=on}} in 20 seconds. Koenigsegg also claims that the acceleration from {{cvt|150–250|km/h|mph|0|abbr=on}} requires 3.9 seconds.<ref name="geneva-2016">{{cite press release |url=http://koenigsegg.com/koenigsegg-presents-agera-rs-production-regera-and-agera-final-at-geneva-2016/ |title=Koenigsegg Presents Production Spec Regera, 'Agera Final' and Agera RS at Geneva 2016 |publisher=Koenigsegg |date=29 February 2016 |access-date=2 March 2016 |archive-date=6 March 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160306155010/http://koenigsegg.com/koenigsegg-presents-agera-rs-production-regera-and-agera-final-at-geneva-2016/ |url-status=dead }}</ref>

The Regera has a power-to-weight ratio of {{convert|{{#expr:1110/1590 round 2}}|kW|hp|2|abbr=on}} per kilogram, and the combined ICE and electric motors produce the highest combined peak power and torque output of any other production hybrid car.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.autoevolution.com/news/production-cars-with-the-highest-torque-numbers-for-2017-119707.html|title=Production Cars with the Highest Torque Numbers For 2017|last=Oagana|first=Alex|date=11 August 2017|website=Autoevolution|access-date=20 March 2018|archive-date=21 March 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180321192643/https://www.autoevolution.com/news/production-cars-with-the-highest-torque-numbers-for-2017-119707.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

== World record ==
On 23 September 2019, the Regera broke the {{convert|0-400-0|km/h|mph|abbr=on}} acceleration record for a production car with a time of 31.49 seconds.<ref name="Koenigsegg-400-record">{{Cite web |url=https://www.koenigsegg.com/koenigsegg-sets-new-0-400-0-km-h-world-record/ |title=Koenigsegg sets new 0-400-0 km/h world record |author=<!--Staff--> |publisher=Koenigsegg |location=Sweden |date=27 September 2019 |access-date=29 September 2019 |archive-date=23 October 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191023235117/https://www.koenigsegg.com/koenigsegg-sets-new-0-400-0-km-h-world-record/ |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.thedrive.com/news/30051/koenigsegg-regera-breaks-0-249-0-mph-world-record-with-31-49-second-run |title=Koenigsegg Regera Breaks 0-249-0 MPH World Record With 31.49-Second Run |last=Tsui |first=Chris |work=The Drive |location=US |date=27 September 2019 |access-date=30 September 2019 |archive-date=28 September 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190928212122/https://www.thedrive.com/news/30051/koenigsegg-regera-breaks-0-249-0-mph-world-record-with-31-49-second-run |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.motor1.com/news/373253/koenigsegg-regera-0-400-0-record/ |title=Koenigsegg Regera Breaks The 0-249-0 MPH (0-400-0 KPH) Record |last=Padeanu |first=Adrian |work=Motor1 |location=US |access-date=30 September 2019 |archive-date=27 September 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190927122442/https://www.motor1.com/news/373253/koenigsegg-regera-0-400-0-record/ |url-status=live }}</ref> This is 1.8 seconds faster than the previous record, also held by Koenigsegg but set by the [[Koenigsegg Agera RS|Agera RS]]. The record was set in [[:sv:Råda flygbas|Råda Military Airfield]] in [[Lidköping]], Sweden with a car driven by factory test driver Sonny Persson.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://jalopnik.com/koenigsegg-regera-proves-it-only-needs-one-gear-to-set-1838528086|title=Koenigsegg Regera Proves It Only Needs One Gear To Set 0-400-0 Record|last=Westbrook|first=Justin|date=27 September 2019|website=Jalopnik|access-date=29 September 2019|archive-date=29 September 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190929192811/https://jalopnik.com/koenigsegg-regera-proves-it-only-needs-one-gear-to-set-1838528086|url-status=live}}</ref>

During the record attempt, the car accelerated from 0–400{{Nbsp}}km/h in 22.87 seconds over a distance of {{convert|1,613.2|m|ft|1|abbr=on}} and decelerated to a controlled stop in 8.62 seconds over a distance of {{convert|435.26|m|ft|1|abbr=on}}.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.topgear.com/car-news/supercars/koenigsegg-just-set-new-0-249mph-0-world-record |title=Koenigsegg just set a new 0-249mph-0 world record |last=Pattni |first=Vijay |work=Top Gear |location=UK |date=27 September 2019 |access-date=30 September 2019 |archive-date=30 September 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190930073130/https://www.topgear.com/car-news/supercars/koenigsegg-just-set-new-0-249mph-0-world-record |url-status=live }}</ref> The total distance used for the record was {{convert|2,048.46|m|ft|1|abbr=on}}. The run was conducted over a combination of runway and taxiway due to insufficient track length and as a safety precaution.<ref name="Koenigsegg-400-record" />

On 16 June 2023, the Regera again broke the 0–400–0{{Nbsp}}km/h acceleration record with an improved time of 28.81 seconds.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Pattni |first=Vijay |date=16 June 2023 |title=Koenigsegg Regera reclaims 0-400-0km/h record from Rimac Nevera |url=https://www.topgear.com/car-news/supercars/koenigsegg-regera-reclaims-0-400-0kmh-record-rimac-nevera |website=TopGear}}</ref> The new record attempt came one month after the Regera's first record was broken by the [[Rimac Nevera]], which achieved a time of 29.93 seconds.<ref>{{Cite web |last=MacKenzie |first=Angus |date=17 May 2023 |title=Electric Rimac Nevera Claims 23 Records in a Day, Sets Quickest 0-249 MPH Time |url=https://www.motortrend.com/news/rimac-nevera-ev-supercar-23-records-quickest-0-249-mph-time/ |website=MotorTrend |access-date=16 June 2023 |archive-date=25 May 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230525204435/https://www.motortrend.com/news/rimac-nevera-ev-supercar-23-records-quickest-0-249-mph-time/ |url-status=live }}</ref> The record was set at [[Örebro Airport]] by Koenigsegg development driver Markus Lundh, who cited improvements in the track surface and the new Michelin Cup 2 R tyres as reasons for the faster run.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Calin |first=Razvan |date=16 June 2023 |title=Watch This Koenigsegg Regera Casually Busting the 0-250-0 MPH One-Month-Fresh Record |url=https://www.autoevolution.com/news/watch-this-koenigsegg-regera-casually-busting-the-0-250-0-mph-one-month-fresh-record-216629.html |website=autoevolution}}</ref>

Over the new record run, the car accelerated from 0–400{{Nbsp}}km/h in 20.68 seconds over a distance of {{convert|1,483|m|ft|1|abbr=on}} and decelerated to a controlled stop in 8.13 seconds over a distance of {{convert|422|m|ft|1|abbr=on}}. The total distance used for the record was {{convert|1905|m|ft|1|abbr=on}}.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |last=Padeanu |first=Adrian |date=16 June 2023 |title=Koenigsegg Regera Beats Rimac Nevera With New 0-249-0 MPH Record |url=https://www.motor1.com/news/672402/koenigsegg-regera-0-249-0-mph-record/ |website=motor1.com}}</ref>

